Topic 4 — Derived query methods
Spring parses the method name:
List&lt;Student&gt; findByEmail(String email);
List&lt;Student&gt; findByAgeGreaterThan(Integer age);
List&lt;Student&gt; findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String part);
Optional&lt;Student&gt; findByEmailAndAge(String email, Integer age);
findBy + Email           → WHERE email = ?
findBy + AgeGreaterThan  → WHERE age &gt; ?
And / Or / Between / IsNull / OrderBy...
If the name is wrong, startup often fails fast — good.

Topic 5 — JPQL with @Query
When names get ugly or logic is complex:
@Query("select s from Student s where s.age &gt;= :minAge order by s.name")
List&lt;Student&gt; olderThan(@Param("minAge") Integer minAge);
JPQL uses entity names / fields, not table/column names (unless native).
Update/delete queries need @Modifying + transaction:
@Modifying
@Query("update Student s set s.age = s.age + 1 where s.id = :id")
int bumpAge(@Param("id") Long id);

Native SQL
@Query(value = "select * from students where age &gt; ?1", nativeQuery = true)
List&lt;Student&gt; nativeOlder(Integer age);
Prefer JPQL when portable mapping matters; native when you need DB-specific SQL.

